{"feedback":"Hypothesis: Melanoma patients with obesity (BMI ≥ 30) have a higher prevalence of PTEN somatic mutations than non-obese patients.

Results: Among 250 patients with non-missing height/weight data, 172 were non-obese (BMI < 30) and 78 were obese (BMI ≥ 30). PTEN mutations were observed in 17 non-obese patients (9.9%) and 10 obese patients (12.8%). Fisher’s exact test yielded an odds ratio of 0.75 and p-value = 0.5130, indicating no statistically significant association.

Conclusion: There is no evidence that obesity at diagnosis is associated with an increased frequency of PTEN mutations in this melanoma cohort (p = 0.513).","success":true}
